[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":34},"en/routes/conteudo-do-cubo.tsx",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10,"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-7,"_20":21,"_22":-7,"_23":-7,"_24":25},"tipo","etapa","cubo",{"_11":32,"_13":32,"_30":33},"id","eoline","nome","EOLine (ZZ)","rascunho",false,"html","<p>The first step of <a href=\"/en/glossary#zz\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Zbigniew Zborowski's method: it orients all the edges at the start (EOLine), and F2L is done with just R, U and L, without rotating the…\">ZZ</a> does two things at once: it orients the 12 edges (<a href=\"/en/glossary#eo\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"Edge orientation: each edge turned so that it can go to its place without using F and B.\">EO</a>) and puts the two white edges of the front and back on the bottom, the &quot;line&quot;. After that, everything else is done with just <code>R</code>, <code>U</code> and <code>L</code>: without <code>F</code> or <code>B</code>, your hands barely change position.</p>\n<h2>What an oriented edge is</h2>\n<p>With white on the bottom and green in front, an edge is <strong>oriented</strong> (&quot;good&quot;) when you can take it to its place with just <code>R</code>, <code>L</code>, <code>U</code> and <code>D</code>. To tell, look at just one sticker of each edge:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>On the top and bottom edges, the sticker facing up or down. On the middle ones, the sticker facing front or back.</li>\n<li>If that sticker is <strong>white or yellow</strong>, the edge is good.</li>\n<li>If it&#39;s <strong>green or blue</strong>, the edge is bad.</li>\n<li>If it&#39;s red or orange, look at its other sticker: white or yellow, bad; green or blue, good.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Bad edges always come in even numbers: 0, 2, 4, 6…</p>\n<h2>How to orient</h2>\n<p>A quarter turn of <code>F</code> (or <code>B</code>) flips the 4 edges of that face: the bad ones become good and the good ones become bad. So:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Take the bad edges to the front face, with <code>R</code>, <code>L</code>, <code>U</code> and <code>D</code> (which don&#39;t change anyone&#39;s orientation).</li>\n<li>With 4 bad ones in front, do <code>F</code> (or <code>F&#39;</code>): all 4 become good.</li>\n<li>With only 2 bad ones, putting both in front doesn&#39;t help: <code>F</code> fixes those two and flips the other two. Put <strong>one</strong> bad edge in front and do <code>F</code>: the 3 good ones in front become bad, and now there are 4. Solve as in step 2.</li>\n<li>With 6 or more, solve 4 at a time.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><code>F2</code> and <code>B2</code> don&#39;t change orientation: you can use them freely.</p>\n<h2>The line</h2>\n<p>With all 12 edges good, put the white and green edge on the bottom, in front, and the white and blue one on the bottom, at the back, with just <code>R</code>, <code>L</code>, <code>U</code>, <code>D</code>, <code>F2</code> and <code>B2</code>. At first, do EO first and the line after. With practice, both come together, planned during <a href=\"/en/glossary#inspecao\" class=\"termo-do-glossario\" title=\"The 15 seconds to look at the cube before you start solving, without turning the faces.\">inspection</a>.</p>\n","treino","metodos",[26],"anterior","proximo","grupos",[],{"_13":27,"_28":29,"_30":31},"ZZ","numero",1,"endereco","/3x3/zz#eoline","3x3","/3x3/",{"_35":36,"_37":38},"cubos",[41,42,43,44,45,46],"idiomas",[39,40],"pt","en",{"_11":32,"_13":32},{"_11":53,"_13":53},{"_11":52,"_13":52},{"_11":51,"_13":51},{"_11":49,"_13":50},{"_11":47,"_13":48},"pyraminx","Pyraminx","megaminx","Megaminx","5x5","4x4","2x2"]
